From af2f060156edb5e2808b85f571cd33385327b29f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hung Fan Lin <leolin84200704@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 17:57:30 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] [VP-18796] Read contains_hormones, hormone approval and
 pns_jwt from their sources instead of LIS-Sample

LIS-Shipping called three LIS-Sample routes over the public ingress and
used one field from each:

- GET /patients/orders/operation-settings -> data.contains_hormones, which
  LIS-Sample derives from order-service
  orderTest/getQuestionnaireRequiredMap.has_hormone_questionnaire;
- GET /tests -> whether a hormone-related test is approved; that status
  comes only from results-grpc getTestsResultsRelatedData;
- GET /patients/pns-jwt -> an HS256 token signed with AUTH_JWT_SECRET,
  which this service already holds (same secret per environment).

Each call also made LIS-Sample call back into this service's gRPC
GetSampleId to resolve an id Shipping had already resolved.

LisSampleDirectService reads the three values from their sources:
the questionnaire map with a 3 s timeout and a 5-minute cache (what
LIS-Sample's Redis cache provided), results-grpc through a second
client on the existing LIS_TEST_RESULT_GRPC_URL host (tests-result-info
proto, last entry per test wins as in LIS-Sample), and a locally minted
token with the same claims and six-hour lifetime.

SHIPPING_LIS_SAMPLE_MODE selects the path per call: http (default,
unchanged behaviour), shadow (both paths, answer from LIS-Sample, one
lis_sample_shadow log line per call with equal / differ / both_failed /
old_failed / new_failed), direct (sources only; a failed direct read of
contains_hormones or the approval flag answers false, as LIS-Sample did).
The service is optional in OrdersService and OrdersHormonesService so
existing test modules are unaffected.

Spec: mode resolution, the three-state comparator, resolve() in all three
modes, the questionnaire-map fetch/cache/timeout, hormone approval with
real proto-shaped responses, and the minted token verified with the real
jsonwebtoken against LIS-Sample's claim shape.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
